---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Chapter from '../components/Chapter.astro';
import Term from '../components/Term.astro';
import RuleVsLearn from '../components/games/RuleVsLearn.astro';
import LabelIt from '../components/games/LabelIt.astro';
import FitLine from '../components/games/FitLine.astro';
import DrawBoundary from '../components/games/DrawBoundary.astro';
import Cluster from '../components/games/Cluster.astro';
import Overfit from '../components/games/Overfit.astro';
---

<Base title="Learn ML with Bittu">
  <!-- ————— hero: the board itself ————— -->
  <header class="hero wrap">
    <div class="hero__map">
      <h1>ML</h1>
      <div class="branch">
        <div class="sketch">Supervised<br /><small>learn from answers</small></div>
        <div class="sketch sketch--alt">Unsupervised<br /><small>learn without answers</small></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="hero__intro">
      <p class="tag">a board you can play</p>
      <p class="lede">
        Six mini-games. One pet. You do not read machine learning here, you fiddle with it until it
        clicks, and <b>Bittu</b> tells you what you just did.
      </p>
      <p>Any <Term def="Like this. Click an underlined word anywhere on the board and I will explain it in one breath.">underlined word</Term> is one I can explain. Poke me any time.</p>
      <button class="btn" data-next="1">Start chapter 1 →</button>
    </div>
  </header>

  <!-- ————— 1 ————— -->
  <Chapter n={1} eyebrow="chapter one · the definition" title="What is machine learning?" locked={false} next={2}>
    <div class="grid2">
      <div class="sketch">
        <p><b>Machine learning</b> is the field of study that gives computers the ability to learn
        without being explicitly programmed. <small>— Arthur Samuel, 1959</small></p>
        <p>
          Normal code: a human writes the rules, the computer applies them.<br />
          Machine learning: a human hands over examples, and the computer searches for the rules itself.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>
          The word <Term def="A search. The computer tries many possible rules, scores each one against the data, and keeps whichever scores best. Do that fast enough and it looks like insight.">learning</Term>
          sounds mystical. It is not. It is search, scored by <Term def="Data: the examples you feed the machine. Rows of things that already happened. Without data there is nothing to learn from and you are back to writing rules by hand.">data</Term>.
        </p>
        <p>Prove it to yourself: try to write the rule by hand, then hand the job to Bittu.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <RuleVsLearn />
  </Chapter>

  <!-- ————— 2 ————— -->
  <Chapter n={2} eyebrow="chapter two · the first branch" title="Supervised learning" next={3}>
    <div class="grid2">
      <div class="sketch">
        <p>
          Learning from <Term def="Labelled data: every example comes with the correct answer attached. Photo plus 'cat'. House plus the price it sold for. Message plus 'junk'. Collecting these labels is usually the expensive part of the job.">labelled data</Term>
          — the model trains on input–output pairs, <span class="mono">X (input) → Y (output)</span>, so it can
          predict the output for new, unseen inputs.
        </p>
        <p><b>Analogy:</b> a teacher grading homework. Every question already has the correct answer marked.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>Predicting a house price:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><Term def="Features: the input columns — the facts you already know about a thing. Size, location, number of rooms. Choosing good features is called feature engineering, and it beats fancy algorithms more often than people admit.">Features (X)</Term> — size, location, number of rooms</li>
          <li><Term def="Label: the answer column — the thing you want to predict. During training you know it. In real use you do not, which is the entire point.">Label (Y)</Term> — the actual sale price</li>
          <li>The model learns the mapping, then prices a house it has never seen</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Two flavours come out of this branch, depending on what Y looks like: a number, or a category.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <LabelIt />
  </Chapter>

  <!-- ————— 3 ————— -->
  <Chapter n={3} eyebrow="chapter three · supervised, flavour one" title="Regression (numbers)" next={4}>
    <p>
      When the answer is a quantity on a sliding scale — ₹, kilograms, minutes, degrees —
      you are doing <Term def="Regression: supervised learning where the output is a continuous number. 'How much' and 'how many' questions. Being close counts as partial credit, which is why the error is measured, not just counted.">regression</Term>.
      Fit a line through the dots so the
      <Term def="Loss: one number scoring how wrong the model currently is. Squared error is the usual choice for regression — it punishes a few big misses more than many small ones. Training means making this number go down.">loss</Term>
      is as small as possible. That is the whole job.
    </p>
    <FitLine />
    <p class="footnote">
      <Term def="Gradient descent: look at the slope of the error under your feet, take a small step downhill, repeat. It is how almost every model — from this line to a language model — gets trained. Step too big and you bounce out of the valley.">Gradient descent</Term>
      is just that button, run a thousand times a second.
    </p>
  </Chapter>

  <!-- ————— 4 ————— -->
  <Chapter n={4} eyebrow="chapter three · supervised, flavour two" title="Classification (category)" next={5}>
    <p>
      When the answer is one of a fixed set of buckets — pass or fail, junk or real, cat or dog —
      you are doing <Term def="Classification: supervised learning where the output is a category. Being close is worth nothing; you are either in the right bucket or you are not, so we count accuracy instead of measuring distance.">classification</Term>.
      The model draws a <Term def="Decision boundary: the surface where the model changes its mind. On one side it predicts A, on the other B. In two dimensions it is a line; in a hundred dimensions it is a shape nobody can picture, which is fine — the maths does not care.">decision boundary</Term>
      and everything on one side gets one label.
    </p>
    <DrawBoundary />
    <p class="footnote">
      Careful with <Term def="Accuracy: the share of predictions that were right. It lies when classes are imbalanced — predict 'not fraud' for everything and you can be 99.9% accurate and completely useless. That is why precision, recall and F1 exist.">accuracy</Term>
      as a score. It flatters lazy models when one class is rare.
    </p>
  </Chapter>

  <!-- ————— 5 ————— -->
  <Chapter n={5} eyebrow="chapter four · the other branch" title="Unsupervised learning" next={6}>
    <div class="grid2">
      <div class="sketch sketch--alt">
        <p>
          Learning from <Term def="Unlabelled data: examples with no correct answer attached. Most data in the world looks like this, because labelling it costs human hours.">unlabelled data</Term>
          — the model finds hidden patterns, structures or groupings on its own, with no correct
          answer provided.
        </p>
        <p><b>Analogy:</b> a pile of mixed Lego with no instructions. You would naturally sort it by colour, size or shape. Nobody told you which sorting was right.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>Customer purchase histories, no “segment” column anywhere. The model finds natural
          <Term def="Clustering: grouping points so that members of a group are more similar to each other than to anything outside it. k-means is the classic: pick k centres, assign every point to the nearest, move the centres to the middle of their crowd, repeat.">clusters</Term>
          and you get budget shoppers, premium buyers, seasonal buyers.
        </p>
        <p>
          There is no accuracy score here, because there is nothing to be accurate against. You judge it
          by whether the groups are useful — and you pick <Term def="k: how many clusters you want. The algorithm cannot tell you; you choose it. Settings you choose rather than learn are called hyperparameters.">k</Term> yourself.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Cluster />
  </Chapter>

  <!-- ————— 6 ————— -->
  <Chapter n={6} eyebrow="chapter five · the thing you actually ship" title="Data + algorithm → model">
    <div class="grid2">
      <div class="sketch">
        <p>
          A <Term def="Model: the mathematical function, or set of rules, that the algorithm learned from the data. It takes features X and produces a prediction ŷ. Once trained, it is the brain you deploy — the data and the training code can go home.">model</Term>
          is the mathematical function the algorithm learned from data. It takes features (X) and
          produces a prediction (ŷ). Once trained, it is the brain that handles new, unseen data.
        </p>
        <p class="mono">data + algorithm → model</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>
          Which means the only honest test is data the model has never met: the
          <Term def="Test set: data held back from training and used exactly once, at the end, to estimate real-world performance. Peek at it while tuning and it quietly becomes training data, and your score becomes a lie.">test set</Term>.
          A model that aces its training data and flops on new data has
          <Term def="Overfitting: the model memorised the noise in the training data instead of the pattern underneath. Symptom: training error tiny, test error large. Cures: more data, fewer parameters, regularisation, early stopping.">overfit</Term> —
          memorised instead of learned.
        </p>
        <p>
          The opposite failure is <Term def="Underfitting: the model is too simple to capture the real pattern, so it is bad on training data and test data alike. Cure: more flexibility, better features.">underfitting</Term>.
          Your job is finding the middle.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <Overfit />
  </Chapter>

  <!-- ————— next ————— -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" data-chapter={7} data-locked="true">
    <div class="lock"><span>🔒</span>Finish the board to see what comes next</div>
    <p class="chapter__eyebrow">the rest of the road</p>
    <h2>What Bittu learns next</h2>
    <p>You now have the spine of the roadmap. Each of these is a future chapter on this board:</p>
    <ol class="road">
      <li><b>Data preparation</b> — cleaning, missing values, scaling, encoding categories</li>
      <li><b>Feature engineering</b> — inventing better inputs, which beats a fancier algorithm more often than not</li>
      <li><b>Evaluation</b> — precision, recall, F1, ROC-AUC, and cross-validation instead of one lucky split</li>
      <li><b>Classic algorithms</b> — logistic regression, decision trees, random forests, gradient boosting, SVMs, k-NN</li>
      <li><b>Regularisation and tuning</b> — ridge, lasso, hyperparameter search</li>
      <li><b>Neural networks</b> — stacking simple units until the boundary can bend any way it likes</li>
      <li><b>Reinforcement learning</b> — the third branch: learning from rewards instead of answers</li>
      <li><b>Deployment</b> — serving, monitoring, drift, retraining</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Full map: <a href="https://roadmap.sh/machine-learning" target="_blank" rel="noopener">roadmap.sh/machine-learning</a>.
      Inspiration for this format: <a href="https://ncase.me/trust/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Evolution of Trust</a> by Nicky Case.
    </p>
  </section>

  <footer class="wrap end">
    <p>Built with Astro. Progress lives in your browser only. <button class="btn btn--ghost" data-reset>Wipe the board</button></p>
  </footer>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: var(--gap);
    align-items: center; padding: clamp(30px, 8vh, 80px) 0;
  }
  @media (max-width: 820px) { .hero { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
  .hero__map { text-align: center; }
  .hero__map h1 { font-size: clamp(90px, 17vw, 180px); line-height: .8; margin: 0; }
  .branch { display: flex; gap: 14px; justify-content: center; margin-top: 26px; position: relative; }
  .branch::before {
    content: ''; position: absolute; top: -26px; left: 25%; right: 25%; height: 22px;
    border-top: 2px dashed var(--chalk-faint); border-left: 2px dashed var(--chalk-faint);
    border-right: 2px dashed var(--chalk-faint); border-radius: 12px 12px 0 0;
  }
  .branch .sketch { flex: 1; font-family: var(--display); font-size: 26px; color: var(--chalk); padding: 12px; }
  .branch small { font-family: var(--body); font-size: 13px; color: var(--ink); }
  .lede { font-size: 1.25rem; }
  .mono { font-family: var(--data); font-size: 15px; color: var(--chalk); }
  ul, ol { padding-left: 1.1em; margin: 0 0 .8em; }
  li { margin-bottom: .3em; }
  .footnote { font-size: .95rem; color: rgba(205, 245, 218, .8); margin-top: 14px; }
  .road li { margin-bottom: .5em; }
  .end { padding: 40px 0 90px; font-family: var(--data); font-size: 13px; color: var(--chalk-dim); }
  small { font-size: .8em; opacity: .8; }
</style>
